📖 SECTION 0: GLOSSARY — Every Term Explained

Read this first. Every term below appears somewhere in the document.

Term Plain English Meaning
API "Application Programming Interface" — a way for one website's code to ask another website's server for data. Think of it as a waiter: you (our site) give an order (a request) to the waiter (API), and the kitchen (the other site's database) sends back food (data).
API Key A secret password that identifies us to the API. Without it, the API refuses to serve us.
API Call / Request One single order placed with the waiter. One call returns ONE PAGE of results, not one product, and not the whole database. This is the most misunderstood concept in the whole project — I'll explain it repeatedly.
Rate Limit How fast we're allowed to place orders. E.g., "30 requests per minute" means we can order 30 pages of data every 60 seconds, no faster. Exceed it → the API blocks us temporarily.
Quota How many orders we're allowed in a period (day / month / year / one-time). E.g., "1,000 requests per month" = 1,000 pages per month, then it resets.
Pagination The process of requesting page 1, then page 2, then page 3… because no API gives you the whole database in one call. We write a loop (code that repeats) to fetch page after page.
Scraping Automatically reading data off a public webpage as if a robot were browsing it. Not the same as an official API. Legally gray. May violate a site's Terms of Service.
Aggregator A middleman service that connects to many other services and gives us one unified interface so we don't have to integrate each one separately.
Unified Schema A single, consistent data format. E.g., a product from Amazon and a product from AliExpress both come back with the same field names (title, price, image_url), so our code doesn't need to be rewritten for each platform.
Affiliate Marketing We display a product. A user clicks our special link. They buy on the merchant's site. The merchant pays us a commission. We never touch the money, the product, or the shipping.
Affiliate Link A URL that contains our unique tracking ID. When someone clicks it, a cookie is set in their browser so the merchant knows we sent them.
Cookie Window The time period after a click during which we still get credit if the user buys. Typically 30 days. If they buy after the cookie expires, we get nothing.
Postback An automatic server-to-server message the merchant sends to the affiliate network saying "this sale belongs to affiliate ID #12345." This is how commissions are tracked without us doing anything manually.
Commission Our cut of the sale. E.g., 5% of a $100 product = $5 to us.
Payout Threshold The minimum balance we must accumulate before we can withdraw. E.g., "$10 minimum" means we can't withdraw $9.50.
Payment Hold A delay before commissions become withdrawable. E.g., "60-day hold" means commissions from January 1 become withdrawable around March 1. This exists because of returns/refunds.
COD (Cash on Delivery) The customer pays cash when the package arrives. We cannot offer this as affiliates — only the merchant can.
Dropshipping We list products, customer pays us, we order from a supplier who ships directly to the customer. This is NOT what we're doing. It requires capital, payment processing, and logistics.
Reseller We buy inventory, hold it, ship it, handle customer service. Even more capital-intensive than dropshipping. Not what we're doing.
PSEB Pakistan Software Export Board. A government body. Registering with them classifies our income as "IT export services" and drops our tax rate dramatically.
NTN National Tax Number. Pakistan's tax ID for businesses/individuals earning taxable income. Required before PSEB registration.
W-8BEN A US tax form. Pakistan has a tax treaty (DTAA) with the USA. Filing W-8BEN tells Amazon "tax me at the treaty rate, not the default."
DTAA Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement. A treaty between two countries so you don't pay tax twice on the same income.
KYC "Know Your Customer." Identity verification. Some services require a registered company + corporate email to pass KYC. Students with Gmail accounts get blocked.
RPS "Requests Per Second." A rate limit measured in seconds instead of minutes. 2 RPS = 2 requests every second.
Compute Unit A billing measurement used by some cloud platforms (like Apify). Instead of charging per request, they charge per "unit of computing work done." Heavier tasks burn more units.
Credit-based billing You get a dollar amount of credit (e.g., $5). Each action costs some fraction of that credit. When the credit is gone, it's gone (unless it renews).
Request-based billing You get a number of requests (e.g., 1,000). Each API call uses one. Simpler to plan around than credit-based billing.
SP-API Amazon's official "Selling Partner API." Powerful but heavily restricted. Cannot be mixed with third-party data in the same application without violating Amazon's terms. We are not using it.
Payout The actual money leaving the affiliate network and arriving in our Payoneer account.
Net-30 / NET-30 Payment terms meaning "paid 30 days after the invoice/end of month." A type of payment delay.
Geo Short for "geography" / region. When a provider says "catalogue availability varies by geo," it means "the products available in Pakistan might be different from the products available in the USA."

