🔄 SECTION 2: THE PIVOT — "UNITE ALL STORES INTO ONE"

2.1 New Vision

A single unified storefront that aggregates products from Amazon (premium feel), AliExpress (cheap/free feel), and others — "nothing new, just all in one." The user sees one beautiful website. Behind the scenes, we pull data from many sources.

2.2 The Core Architectural Finding

No single provider covers every platform for both product data AND affiliate data.

This means we cannot just sign up for one service and be done. We need a layered stack — different tools for different jobs, stacked on top of each other.

2.3 The Three-Layer Architecture

Layer 1 — Our Custom Frontend:
Our own website. Our own design. Our own branding. This is the only part the customer ever sees. It looks like Amazon, feels like Amazon, but it's ours.

Layer 2 — Product Data Layer:
Tools that fetch product information (titles, prices, images, descriptions) from Amazon, AliExpress, etc., and normalize it into one consistent format so our frontend can display it uniformly.

Layer 3 — Affiliate/Monetization Layer:
Tools that generate our tracked affiliate links and report commissions back to us.

2.4 The Critical Legal Catch (Amazon SP-API)

Amazon's official API (SP-API) has a rule: you cannot mix SP-API data with third-party data in the same application. If we pull Amazon data officially and also pull AliExpress data from a scraper, and display them side-by-side, we violate Amazon's terms.

Our solution: Do not use Amazon's official SP-API at all. Use only third-party scrapers/aggregators for all data. This keeps us out of Amazon's direct compliance framework entirely.

2.5 The "56 Similar + One FREE on AliExpress" Feature

This is the killer feature we want. When a user views a product, we show:

· "56 similar products"
· "One of these is FREE on AliExpress" (or dramatically cheaper)

How it works technically:

· Visual/image search — send an image to an API, get back visually similar products across platforms.
· Keyword/category matching — search other platforms using the product's title and category.
· Price comparison badges — flag items found cheaper elsewhere.

PROFIT situation: This feature is what makes us different from just "another Amazon clone." It gives users a reason to visit us instead of going straight to Amazon.

LOSE situation: If we can't reliably match products across platforms (because titles differ wildly, or images are watermarked differently), the feature looks broken and users lose trust.

