📐 SECTION 6: WHAT "1 API CALL" ACTUALLY MEANS (CRITICAL)

6.1 The Misconception I Had

I initially thought "1 API call = 1 product" or "1 API call = the whole database." Both are wrong.

6.2 The Correct Understanding

One API call = ONE PAGE of results. Usually 10–100 products depending on the provider.

6.3 The Correct Math

Total product records = (number of calls in the period) × (products per call)

6.4 Your Realistic Monthly Capacity

Amazon (Amazonscraperapi):

· 1,000 requests/month × 16–48 products per page = 16,000–48,000 product records per month
· Minus duplicates, sponsored items, variants that repeat across pages
· Realistic unique catalog: maybe 10,000–30,000 usable unique products/month

AliExpress (BayDeep):

· The key never expires, so time is the only constraint, not quota
· Limited by 30 req/min and avoiding quota-heavy endpoints (search_products, product_detail consume platform quota; use shelf_search for repeated lookups)
· Realistic: you can build a large AliExpress catalog over several weeks, not in one day

6.5 The "Per Day" Confusion (Explained)

I asked: "Is 10 × 1000 calls = 10,000 products per day?"
No. That depends on the quota period:

· 1,000 calls/month × 10 products/call = 10,000 records/MONTH
· 1,000 calls/day × 10 products/call = 10,000 records/DAY
· 1,000 calls one-time × 10 products/call = 10,000 records TOTAL, ever

You must always ask: "Is the quota per day, per month, or one-time?"

6.6 Other Practical Catches

· Records ≠ unique products. Pagination returns duplicates.
· Search APIs often cap total results. E.g., Amazon-style APIs may only let you page through the first 100 results per search query, even if you have more calls left.
· Free tiers reset monthly, not daily — verify each provider's terms.

